    "created_at": "2026-01-07T21:18:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-07T21:35:55Z",
    "body": "I think the relevant thing to explain would be that `about:legacy-compat`, `about:html-kind`, and `about:srcdoc` are **well-known \"about\" URIs** as defined in RFC 6694[^1] (and registered in the corresponding IANA registry[^2]):\n>  A well-known \"about\" URI is a URI that has a well-known token as its <about-token> part.  It is recommended that such URIs be handled in accordance with the specification referenced in the \"about\" URI Tokens registry.\n\nThe specification already cites the RFC, but it would probably be clearer to explain in more detail what precisely is being referred to there, and perhaps also to refer to RFC 6694's statement that \"the `<about-token>` part of the URI ... is not a hierarchical element for a naming authority\"[^3] (i.e., `about:` URIs are never resolvable).\n\n[^1]: S. Moonesamy, ed., \"The 'about' URI Scheme\", RFC 6694 (IETF, August 2012), <https://doi.org/10.17487/rfc6694>, sec. 2.2.1, \"Well-Known 'about' URIs\".\n[^2]: \"'about' URI Tokens\", Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA), last updated 6 September 2016, <https://www.iana.org/assignments/about-uri-tokens>.\n[^3]: RFC 6694, sec. 2.2, \"URI Scheme Semantics\".",
